<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian indenture system</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as girmit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian indenture system was a system of indentured servitude that carried more than 1.6 million workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to European colonies after the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Described by colonial authorities as free migration, it relied on recruits who were often deceived, coerced or kidnapped, and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-tinker">Hugh Tinker</a> called it a new form of slavery. It began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> in 1834 and spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, Natal, Réunion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, where labourers worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations under harsh discipline and criminal penalties. Export to Natal ended in 1911 and to the other colonies in 1917, leaving large Indian diasporas.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s Regulations of 1837 set the conditions for sending Indian labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. The would-be emigrant and his emigration agent had to appear before an officer named by the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> with the terms of the contract in writing. Service was to last exactly five years, renewable for further five-year terms, with a return to the port of departure at the end, and each emigrant ship had to carry a medical officer.</p><p>A campaign like the one against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> soon arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>: a committee appointed on 1 August 1838 heard reports of abuses, and on 29 May 1839 overseas manual labour was prohibited, on pain of a 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a> fine or three months in jail. Under pressure from planters, the British Government allowed emigration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> again on 2 December 1842; the first ship left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> on 23 January 1843, and 30,218 men and 4,307 women entered Mauritius during that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Emigration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and Demerara was legalised on 16 November 1844, and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, St Lucia, St Kitts and St Vincent. The first ship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> on 16 November 1860, the beginning of the Indian South African community. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to recruit 6,000 labourers a year for Réunion from 25 July 1860. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> gained the right to recruit for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> by handing old forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> to the British, and the first ship reached Suriname in June 1873.</p><p>Between 1842 and 1870, 525,482 Indians emigrated to the British and French colonies, 351,401 of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> joined the system in 1879. The ban on emigration to Natal came from a bill tabled in February 1910 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gopal-krishna-gokhale">Gopal Krishna Gokhale</a>, a moderate Congress leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Regulations of 1864 required recruits to appear before a magistrate in their own district, licensed recruiters, paid agents by salary rather than commission and fixed 25 women for every 100 men. The colonies still passed laws against the labourers: a Demerara ordinance of 1864 made absence from work a crime, and Mauritian laws of 1867 obliged former labourers to carry passes. Under the agreement of 1912 they worked nine hours on five weekdays and five hours on Saturday.</p><p>Labourers were housed in crowded barracks of wood or mud, where cholera, malaria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and dysentery spread quickly; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> the yearly death rate among new arrivals sometimes passed 20%. Overseers enforced discipline with whipping and beatings. Some ships carried as few as one woman for every 40 men, and missionary and colonial reports recorded widespread rape and violence against women.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian indenture system” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
